<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paris Peace Conference (1919–1920)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Paris Peace Conference · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> Peace Conference was the meeting of the victorious Allied powers after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, opened in January 1919 and dominated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It drew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the treaties of Saint-Germain, Neuilly, Trianon and Sèvres, created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and redrew the map of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920.jpg" alt="Paris Peace Conference (1919–1920)" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Noël Dorville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris_Peace_Conference.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1917 US president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> commissioned about 150 academics to research the topics likely to arise in peace talks and to develop principles for the negotiations. Their work was summarised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a>, which became the basis of the terms of the German surrender and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">Armistice of 11 November 1918</a>. The conference brought together diplomats from 32 countries and nationalities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> were given no voice in the deliberations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was represented, though not by its actual government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The conference formally opened on 18 January 1919 at the Quai d'Orsay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, a date chosen because it was the anniversary of the proclamation of William I as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-emperor">German Emperor</a> in 1871. Delegates were assigned to 52 commissions, which held 1,646 sessions on topics ranging from prisoners of war to undersea cables. The five great powers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, controlled it, and the Big Four leaders, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-clemenceau">Georges Clemenceau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a>, Wilson and Vittorio Emanuele Orlando, met informally 145 times and agreed all major decisions before they were ratified.</p><p>Clemenceau's chief aim was to weaken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> militarily, strategically and economically. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> sought the territories promised in the secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a>. Japan proposed a racial equality clause for the League's Covenant on 13 February and claimed Germany's concessions in Shantung. The Western powers transferred those concessions to Japan, which sparked student protests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> on 4 May, later known as the May Fourth Movement; the Chinese delegation was the only one that did not sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The main result was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which had 15 chapters and 440 clauses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-guilt-clause">Article 231</a> of the treaty placed responsibility for the war on &quot;the aggression of Germany and her allies&quot;, a provision that humiliated German leaders and citizens, and Germany stopped paying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> after 1931, having delivered only a small portion. German and Ottoman overseas possessions passed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">mandates</a> chiefly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and new boundaries were drawn, sometimes after plebiscites. Delegations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, Latvia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> won recognition of their independence, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, Ireland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> were effectively ignored.</p><p>The senior statesmen stopped working personally on the conference in June 1919, but historian Michael Neiberg notes that the formal peace process did not end until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> of July 1923. The settlement is considered one of the great watersheds of 20th-century geopolitics, and its exclusion of the defeated powers later gave rise to political resentments that lasted decades.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paris Peace Conference (1919–1920)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 7 May 1919 the Supreme Council of the <b>Paris Peace Conference</b> awarded all of German East Africa to Britain, over Belgium's strenuous objections.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-east-africa">German East Africa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Italy was counted among the great powers from the late nineteenth century and sat among the victors at the <b>Paris Peace Conference</b> in 1919, where frustration over its gains fed the idea of a mutilated victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Paris Peace Conference</b> of 1919 Patriarch Elias Peter Hoayek won an enlarged territory for the Maronites, including Muslim and Druze districts, and after Faisal's Arab Kingdom of Syria fell at the Battle of Maysalun in 1920 the San Remo Conference placed Syria and Lebanon under French…</blockquote><a href="/empire/lebanese-republic">Lebanese Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 4 May 1919 students in Beijing protested against the transfer of German rights in Shandong to Japan at the <b>Paris Peace Conference</b>, and the May Fourth Movement fed modern Chinese nationalism and, in 1921, the founding of the Chinese Communist Party.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It framed the armistice and the <b>Paris Peace Conference</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-8">8 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leaders hoped for union with Russia or with the Rusyns of Czechoslovakia and sent delegates to the <b>Paris Peace Conference</b>, without success.</blockquote><a href="/state/lemko-rusyn-republic">Lemko-Rusyn Republic</a></li></ul></section>
